ion 2.4 using the weighted residual loss of Equation 3.3 with\nL505@P5: \u000f = 100, for 3 × 105iterations of gradient descent under exactly the same hyper-parameter settings. The results of this\nL506@P5: experiment are summarized in Figure 3. One can see that the predicted solution achieves an excellent agreement with\nL507@P5: the ground truth, yielding an approximation error of 1.43e − 03 measured in the relative L\nL508@P5: 2 norm. The left panel of\nL509@P5: Figure 4 presents the convergence of the different loss function components, which is evidently much better than the\nL510@P5: one presented in Figure 2. Here we note that no other modifications between the two cases exist, besides the use of the\nL511@P5: proposed weighted residual loss of Equation 3.3.\nL512@P5: In fact, if in conjunction with the weighted residual loss we also employ a more powerful architecture for this example,\nL513@P5: such as the modified MLP [12] described in section 4, then we can achieve an even more accurate result with a resulting\nL514@P5: relative L\nL515@P5: 2\nL516@P5: error of 1.39e − 04. Additional detailed visual assessments for this example are provided in Appendix D.\nL517@P5: Finally, in Table 1 we provide the accuracy reported for this problem by existing approaches in the literature [14, 16, 25].\nL518@P5: It is evident that the proposed methodology outperforms the best reported result of competing approaches by a factor of\nL519@P5: ∼10-100x. This is a strong indication of the significance and necessity of respecting causality in training PINNs.\nL520@P5: A stopping criterion for assessing training convergence: To understand the effect of the residual weights {wi},\nL521@P5: we present the temporal residual loss and weights at different iterations of gradient descent in the middle and right panel\nL522@P5: of Figure 4 and Figure 16. We observe that the initial temporal weights are all zero except for t = 0, implying that only\n------------------------------------