gnitude of the residual weights {wi} can provide an effective stopping criterion for\nL598@P6: assessing the convergence of a PINNs model during training. Specifically, one can choose to terminate training of\nL599@P6: mini wi > δ, for some chosen threshold parameter δ ∈ (0, 1). As we will see in section 5, this stopping criterion not\nL600@P6: only helps to train a PINNs model faster, but it actually yields trained models with superior predictive accuracy.\nL601@P6: Sensitivity on the causality parameter \u000f: Here we must note that the results obtained using the proposed weighted\nL602@P6: residual loss do exhibit some sensitivity to the causality parameter \u000f in Equation 3.2. Choosing a very small \u000f can\nL603@P6: prevent the network from effectively minimizing the latter temporal residuals. On the other hand, choosing a large\nL604@P6: \u000f value can result in a more difficult optimization problem, because the temporal residuals at earlier times have to\nL605@P6: decrease to a very small value in order to activate the latter temporal weights. This may be hard to achieve in some cases\nL606@P6: due to limited network capacity in minimizing the target residuals. In order to avoid tedious hyper-parameter tuning, we\nL607@P6: employ an annealing strategy for adjusting \u000f using an increasing sequence of values {\u000fi}\nL608@P6: k\nL609@P6: i=1, which gradually increases\nL610@P6: the strength with which the PDE residual constraint is enforced. As we will see in section 5, we empirically observe\nL611@P6: that this choice yields the best results in practice.\nL612@P6: Fitting the initial data: In the spirit of respecting causality, one may recognize that all temporal residuals should be\nL613@P6: minimized only if the network can first accurately fit the initial data. Therefore, we may treat the initial loss Lic as a\nL614@P6: special temporal residual at t = 0 and incorporate it into the weighted residual loss of Equation 3.1 in the same manner.\nL615@P6-7: 7A PREPRINT - MARCH 16, 2022\nL616@P7: Causal training for PINNs: Based on the above remarks, Algorithm 1 presents a general causal training algorithm\nL617@P7: for PINNs. Specifically, it summarizes the proposed re-formulation of the residual and initial conditions loss, the\nL618@P7: annealing scheme for the \u000f parameter, and the stopping criterion for terminating the training upon the convergence of\nL619@P7: the temporal weights wi. Accompanying Algorithm 1, here we present a few additional remarks worth discussing.\nL620@P7: 1. Although in this work we have limited our attention to PDEs with periodic boundary conditions that can be enforced\nL621@P7: in an exact manner (see section 4 for more details), the proposed causal training algorithm can be adapted to also\n"
